n the full suite or the `html` tests. Everything else below is **read** from the diff.\n\n## 1. Mismatches\n\n- **Undisclosed drop: the \"Uncommitted work\" entry point (read).** The summary says only the wip row's stale warning and glossed mark were dropped, which implies the row stays. In the new code it does not. `listRefs` returns only branches, remotes and `defaultBase`, and `picker.js` has no wip option. Snapshotted work is reachable only by opening `/` with nothing chosen (a default, not a choice) or by typing `wip` into the revision box. The README's \"opens first\" wording is accurate; the summary is not. This should be listed under \"Dropped on purpose.\"\n- **`error-page`'s \"Back to the default view\" link can loop (read).** When there are no branches and a detached HEAD, `/` throws, the error page links to `/`, and that throws again. TODO.md records this, but the group's risk notes mention only the empty-repo case.\n\n## 2. Misgraded necessity\n\n- **`sticky-bar` is labelled `supporting`; it looks incidental (read).** The author's own notes say \"the task itself does not require a sticky bar.\" The group also changes the shared desk CSS, so `git gloss html` gets a sticky top bar, a new behaviour there and not only a rail fix. The rail-clipping bug predates this change in `html`. The `ResizeObserver` and `--top-h` plumbing are needed only if the bar is sticky. Relabel it `incidental`, or split out the rail fix as a fix in its own right.\n- **`desk-route` as `required` and `picker` as `required` are fair.** `desk-bar` is fair too.\n- **`tests` as `supporting` is fair.** The browser tests cover the picker and the sticky bar, so they serve several groups.\n\n## 3. Removal candidates\n\n- **`sticky-bar`** (see above). Removing it leaves the rail's last rows possibly unreachable at scroll 0 in both `ary` and `html`, and `html` keeps its pre-existing bug. The cost is a visible layout bug that predates this change, not a regression from it.\n- **The \"Back to the default view\" link** in `error-page`. It adds little, and it is the source of the loop in section 1.\n\n## 4. Simplifications\n\n- **`deskPage` head/base resolution (read).** The four-branch `if/else` ladder with `notice` set inside it is the hardest part to follow. It could be one function that returns `{ head, base, notice }`. The redirect-or-render decision could then be a separate step. This is a readability change only.\n- **`defaultRev` (read).** It runs `rev-list --count base..head` over the whole range before checking the 200-commit cap. Counting with a bound (`-n 201`) would avoid walking very large histories on every bare `/` request.\n\n## 5. Missing risks\n\n- **Stale URL after a notice (read).** With a notice, `/` does not redirect, so the address keeps the deleted branch name. Each reload repeats the notice. This is intended but worth noting.\n- **Base picker and revision box can disagree (read).** After a typed range such as `main..feature`, the base picker can show \"(none)\" while the revision is a range. Harmless, but not called out.\n- **Each `picker()` adds a permanent `document` `mousedown` listener (read).** Harmless because the page is short-lived, but it is a leak in a long-lived session.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
